    "result_text": "Done. Full suite is green (`AcceptanceTest` is the only test class, and it passes, as does the build).\n\n## What I implemented\n\nThe `sharesHousehold` flag previously only *skipped* the duplicate-household rejection. Now, when true, the new owner joins the existing same-lastName/same-address household and every member shares one stable `householdId`, which is returned.\n\n**Changes, each in the place it belongs:**\n\n1. **`Households` helper** (`rest/function/owner/Households.java`) \u2014 the natural home for household logic. Added:\n   - `matches(owner, lastName, address)` \u2014 canonical (case/whitespace-insensitive) household membership check.\n   - `id(lastName, address)` \u2014 derives the shared id (`H-<12 upper-hex of SHA-256(key)>`). It's *stable* by construction: derived purely from the canonical household key, so all members compute the same value with no coordination.\n\n2. **`EnsureUniqueHousehold`** \u2014 refactored to reuse `Households.matches` instead of duplicating the normalize-and-compare loop.\n\n3. **`AssignHousehold`** (new function, single responsibility) \u2014 when `sharesHousehold` is true and an existing owner already occupies the household, stamps that shared id on both the new owner and every existing member (saving them). Owners not in a shared household keep a null id.\n\n4. **Pipeline** (`owners.POST.yml`) \u2014 inserted an `assignHousehold` step after `build`, under the same `transaction` governance.\n\n5. **Persistence & DTO plumbing:**\n   - `Owner` entity: `household_id` column + accessors + `toString`.\n   - All four DB schemas (`hsqldb`, `h2`, `mysql`, `postgres`): added the `household_id` column.\n   - `openapi.yml`: added read-only `householdId` to the `Owner` schema, so it's returned.\n   - `OwnerMapper`: `toOwnerDto` maps it automatically; the body\u2192entity `toOwner(OwnerFieldsDto)` ignores it (assigned by the function, never from the request).\n\nThe acceptance test's two shared-household joiners now return the same non-blank `householdId`.",
